L["baptism"] = """Jesus was about thirty years old. He had grown up in Nazareth, worked as a carpenter, and lived a quiet life. Now the time had come to begin his work. He left Galilee and traveled about sixty miles to the Jordan River, where John was baptizing. He came to be baptized by him.

John tried to stop him. I need to be baptized by you, he said, and do you come to me? John knew who Jesus was. He knew that Jesus had no sins to confess, and he felt that it should be the other way around. But Jesus replied, let it be so now. It is proper for us to do this to fulfill all righteousness. Then John agreed.

Jesus didn't need to repent. He was choosing to stand with the people he came to save. He went down into the water with sinners, and he took his place beside them. It was a sign of what he would do on the cross.

As soon as Jesus was baptized, he went up out of the water. At that moment heaven was opened, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and alighting on him. And a voice from heaven said, this is my Son, whom I love. With him I am well pleased.

All three persons of the Trinity are present in this scene. The Son is in the water, the Spirit comes down like a dove, and the Father speaks from heaven. And the Father's words were spoken before Jesus had preached a sermon, healed anyone, or worked a miracle. God said he loved his Son and was pleased with him before any of that happened.

John later told people what he had seen. I saw the Spirit come down from heaven as a dove and remain on him. I would not have known him, except that the one who sent me to baptize with water told me, the man on whom you see the Spirit come down and remain is the one who will baptize with the Holy Spirit. I have seen and I testify that this is God's Chosen One.

Why does this matter? The baptism marks the start of Jesus' public work. It confirms who he is, the beloved Son, and it shows he was anointed with the Holy Spirit for his mission. It also shows that Jesus is willing to go all the way down to where we are.

What happened after? Right after his baptism, the Spirit led Jesus into the wilderness. He fasted forty days and was tempted by the devil. The first temptation began with the words, if you are the Son of God. The enemy tried to make him doubt what the Father had just said. Jesus answered each one with Scripture and didn't give in. Then he began to preach, call disciples, heal the sick, and announce the kingdom of God.

What does this mean for us today? Christian baptism echoes this scene. When we are baptized into Christ, we are joined to him. What the Father said about the Son is also what he says about those who are in him. You are mine, I love you. And that comes first, before we earn anything. It also shows that following Jesus starts with grace, not with a performance.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus is the beloved Son of God, and that he came down into the water with us. And it shows the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit working together to save the world."""
